Q: javax.swing does not exist A: <p>Read the fine manual.</p>
<ol>
<li>Specifically, read the Javadoc for the class <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.desktop/javax/swing/JOptionPane.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>JOptionPane</code></a>.</li>
<li>Copy-paste the fully-qualified name for that class.</li>
<li>Paste name into your <code>import</code> statement.</li>
<li>Re-compile.</li>
</ol>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>import javax.swing.JOptionPane;
</code></pre>
<p>Read <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/uiswing/index.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><em>Java Tutorials</em></a> by Oracle, free of charge. See simple <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/displayCode.html?code=https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/uiswing/examples/start/HelloWorldSwingProject/src/start/HelloWorldSwing.java" rel="nofollow noreferrer">example Swing app</a> with an <code>import</code> line.</p>
<p>Your <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Integrated_development_environment" rel="nofollow noreferrer">IDE</a> should be helping you with this, automatically generating the <code>import</code> statements.</p>
<p><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swing_(Java)" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Swing</a> has been built into every version of Java since December 8, 1998. So there is no way that <code>JOptionPane</code> is actually missing.</p>
<p>If your app is <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Java_Platform_Module_System" rel="nofollow noreferrer">modularized</a>, your <code>module-info.java</code> file must contain <code>requires java.desktop;</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>module com.example.myswingapp {

    // Core Java SE APIs (Collections, IO, etc.) - often implied but good to be explicit
    requires java.base;

    // Swing and AWT UI components
    requires java.desktop;
}
</code></pre>
